 “greater prophets” of the Old Testament, son of Hilkiah, a priest of Anathoth (Jer. 1:1; 32:6). He was called to the prophetical office when still young (1:6), in the thirteenth year of Josiah (B.C. 628).","verses":124,"father":"Hilkiah","mother":"","v":[1]},{"id":"cyrus_968","name":"Cyrus","also":"","summary":"(Heb. Ko’resh), the celebrated “King of Persia” (Elam) who was conqueror of Babylon, and issued the decree of liberation to the Jews (Ezra 1:1, 2).","verses":19,"father":"","mother":"","v":[1,2,7,8]},{"id":"benjamin_463","name":"Benjamin","also":"Ben-oni","summary":"The younger son of Jacob by Rachel (Gen. 35:18). His birth took place at Ephrath, on the road between Bethel and Bethlehem, at a short distance from the latter place.","verses":157,"father":"Israel","mother":"Rachel","v":[5]},{"id":"nebuchadnezzar_2167","name":"Nebuchadnezzar","also":"Nebuchadrezzar","summary":"In the Babylonian orthography Nabu-kudur-uzur, which means “Nebo, protect the crown!” or the “frontiers.” In an inscription he styles himself “Nebo’s favourite.”","verses":88,"father":"","mother":"","v":[7]},{"id":"zerubbabel_3054","name":"Zerubbabel","also":"Sheshbazzar, Zorobabel","summary":"The seed of Babylon, the son of Salathiel or Shealtiel (Hag. 1:1; Zorobabel, Matt. 1:12); called also the son of Pedaiah (1 Chr. 3:17-19), i.e., according to a frequent usage of the word “son;” the grandson or the nephew of Salathiel.","verses":27,"father":"Pedaiah","mother":"","v":[8,11]},{"id":"mithredath_2097","name":"Mithredath","also":"","summary":"The “treasurer” of King Cyrus (Ezra 1:8).","verses":1,"father":"","mother":"","v":[8]}],"places":[{"id":"persia_938","name":"Persia","kind":"Region (Country)","lat":34.79831,"lon":48.51497,"summary":"An ancient empire, extending from the Indus to Thrace, and from the Caspian Sea to the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf. The Persians were originally a Medic tribe which settled in Persia, on the eastern side of the Persian Gulf.","verses":25,"v":[1,2,8]},{"i